# CI: apply 0062 AND 0063 to the migration chain, in one patch.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------
# Two migrations are being added to the GUS chain, on two sibling PRs:
#
#   0062  the ingredient sourcing vocabulary  (feat/0062-sourcing-vocabulary)
#   0063  record 0061 in the ledger         (db/0063-record-0061-in-the-ledger)
#
# Each adds its migration to TWO of the three lists:
#
#     scripts/ci-migration-chain.sh   (the Nightly database)
#     scripts/repro-ci-db.sh          (the LOCAL reproduction)
#
# The THIRD list, `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (the CI database), is the
# one the agent cannot push -- the GitHub App (`gustale-bot`) has NO
# `workflows` scope, and GitHub refuses the push outright:
#
#     ! [remote rejected] ... (refusing to allow a GitHub App to create
#       or update workflow `.github/workflows/ci.yml` without
#       `workflows` permission)
#
# So the workflow change ships as a FILE the owner applies.
#
# WHY ONE PATCH AND NOT TWO
# --------------------------
# The two steps land in the SAME place in `ci.yml`, so two separate
# patches would CONFLICT when applied one after the other. This patch
# carries BOTH steps, in chain order, and applies in one go to a
# pristine `main`:
#
#     git checkout main && git pull
#     git apply docs/ci-migration-chain-0062-0063.patch
#     git commit -m "ci: apply 0062 and 0063 to the migration chain"
#     git push
#
# The chain order matters: the validator
# (`scripts/validate-ci-migration-chain.py`) reads the three lists as a
# sequence, and 0063 records 0061 in the ledger, so it must come after
# 0061 has created the alias table its P4 proof inspects.
#
# VERIFYING IT AFTER IT LANDS
# ---------------------------
#     python3 scripts/validate-ci-migration-chain.py
#
# should report:
#
#     CI migration chain OK (63 migrations, all three lists agree)
#
# NOTE ON THE ANCHOR
# ------------------
# The hunk below is anchored AFTER the 0061 step (`INGREDIENT_ALIASES_OK`)
# and BEFORE "The PER-DISH backfill assertions". It used to be anchored
# after 0060, which was correct while 0061 lived in the unmerged PR
# #266. PR #266 is now merged, so 0061 is in `ci.yml` and the old
# anchor no longer matches. Regenerate this patch if `ci.yml` moves
# again:
#
#     git diff --no-color .github/workflows/ci.yml > docs/ci-migration-chain-0062-0063.patch
#
diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index 573c315..beb7304 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -1349,12 +1349,40 @@ jobs:
       - name: Apply the ingredient aliases table
         run: |
           PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
             -f db/migrations/0061_ingredient_aliases.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0061.log
           bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the ingredient aliases table" "INGREDIENT_ALIASES_OK" /tmp/m0061.log
 
+      # ── 0062: the ingredient sourcing vocabulary ─────────────────────────
+      #
+      # The 21 reviewed ingredient rows (GUS-1108), all `draft`. It is
+      # additive -- it writes 21 rows into `ingredients` and touches
+      # nothing else -- and it comes after 0055, because the availability
+      # rows it exists for live in the tables 0055 creates.
+      - name: Apply the ingredient sourcing vocabulary
+        run: |
+          PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
+            -f db/migrations/0062_ingredients_sourcing_vocabulary.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0062.log
+          bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "the ingredient sourcing vocabulary" "0062: all 6 proofs passed" /tmp/m0062.log
+
+      # ── 0063: record 0061 in the ledger ───────────────────────────
+      #
+      # 0061 was applied to live on 2026-11-19 without writing its own
+      # ledger row. An applied migration is frozen (Rule 11), so the gap
+      # is closed in a NEW number rather than by editing 0061.
+      #
+      # It writes ONE row -- the ledger entry for 0061 -- and nothing
+      # else. Its P4 proof checks that the alias table 0061 created still
+      # holds exactly its one row: a ledger migration must not disturb
+      # the data.
+      - name: Record 0061 in the ledger
+        run: |
+          PGPASSWORD=gustale psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U postgres -d gustale_ci -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
+            -f db/migrations/0063_record_0061_in_the_ledger.sql 2>&1 | tee /tmp/m0063.log
+          bash scripts/check-ci-markers.sh "record 0061 in the ledger" "GUS_LEDGER_0061_OK" /tmp/m0063.log
+
       # The PER-DISH backfill assertions. LAST, because they assert the
       # state the whole chain produces: the reference dishes'
       # association rows AND the journey places in the editorial queue.
       #
       # 0037 asserts its outcome in AGGREGATE: the counts against the
       # legacy tables. Both sides of that comparison come from the same
